Change per passage (what the representation did):
   1   2.5  ~~
          Socrates depicts chained cave-prisoners who can perceive only shadows and echoes,
          establishing them as like ourselves in enlightenment.
   2 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows that release from illusion begins with painful glare, confusion, and
          resistance, as the prisoner clings to familiar shadows before being dazzled by greater
          realities up to the sun.
   3 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates traces the freed prisoner's gradual ascent from shadows and reflections to
          the sun itself as cause of the visible world, leaving him to pity his former den and
          despise its shadow-honours.
   4 47.25  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows the returning philosopher blinded, ridiculed, and threatened with
          death, then explicitly decodes the cave as the visible world and crowns the ascent
          with the Idea of the Good.
   5  30.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates excuses the enlightened philosopher's unwillingness to return and ridiculous
          blindness in human affairs, then teaches that dazzlement comes from either entering
          light or darkness and should be diagnosed rather than mocked.
   6  20.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates rejects teachers who claim to insert knowledge into the soul, arguing that
          education is instead the art of turning the soul's innate power from becoming toward
          being and the Good.
   7  18.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates argues that keen natures freed from sensual drag could see truth sharply, so
          the founders must compel those who reach the Good to descend again and govern.
   8  20.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates answers Glaucon's charge of injustice by arguing that educated philosophers
          owe ruling service to the whole State, whose reluctant rulers will govern best.
   9  19.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates concludes that just philosophic pupils will accept ruling as a stern
          necessity, and only rulers given a better life of philosophy and rich in virtue can
          keep the State well-ordered.
Constructed meaning c03_1 (passage 4, inferred): The philosopher's return to darkness leaves him blind, contemptible in judging shadows, and exposed to murderous rejection by those he would free.
    built from: Returnee blinded by darkness (provides the initial incapacity: re-entry into
    darkness blinds the freed prisoner); Ridiculed shadow competitor (provides the social
    consequence: dazzled incompetence at shadows makes ascent seem worthless and ridiculous);
    Death threat to liberator (provides the violent escalation: prisoners would kill anyone
    attempting further liberation)
Constructed meaning c03_2 (passage 4, inferred): The sun that seemed the ultimate guardian and end of vision is itself only a visible image of the Idea of the Good, which is the true last object and universal author.
    built from: Sun as World Guardian (provides the sun's apparent ultimacy as guardian and
    cause of all visible things); Cave to intellect mapping (relocates the fire/sun to the
    visible world and the ascent to the soul's journey to intellect, opening a level above the
    sun); Good as ultimate source (provides the higher ultimacy: the Idea of the Good seen last
    as universal author of beauty, right, light, reason and truth)
Constructed meaning c04_1 (passage 5, inferred): The retreat from glaring light toward familiar visibles and the blinded return to darkness are opposite dazzles from going into light and coming out of light that call for asking about the soul's direction rather than laughing at its confusion.
    built from: Retreat from glaring light (provides the ascending case of pain-driven turning
    away from light toward familiar visibles); Returnee blinded by darkness (provides the
    descending case of blindness on sudden replacement in darkness); Two kinds of blindness
    (provides the general scheme of two opposite causes of bewilderment for bodily and mental
    eyes); Suspend mockery and diagnose (provides the normative consequence to suspend laughter
    and first ask about direction)
Constructed meaning c04_2 (passage 5, inferred): The philosopher's unwillingness to descend to human affairs and his ridiculous blinking when forced to dispute about images of justice are excusable, even happy, effects of coming out of brightness rather than proof that ascent ruined his sight.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides unwillingness to descend to human
    affairs from longing to dwell above); Returner's courtroom blindness (provides concrete
    ridiculous failure when compelled to dispute about images of justice); Ridiculed shadow
    competitor (provides prisoners' mocking verdict that ascent destroyed sight, which is now
    overturned); Happy descent versus pity (provides revaluation of descending dazzle as happy
    versus ascending dazzle as pitiable)
Constructed meaning c05_1 (passage 6, inferred): The chained condition and dragged ascent depict not absence of sight requiring implantation but misdirection of an innate learning power requiring whole-soul turning from becoming toward being.
    built from: Chained Underground Prisoners (provides the fixed forward gaze now read as wrong
    direction rather than blindness); Dragged ascent to sun (provides the painful forced ascent
    now read as image of educational reorientation); Innate power of learning (provides the
    premise that capacity for learning already exists in the soul); Art of easy conversion
    (provides the redefinition of education as reorienting existing sight turned wrong rather
    than implanting sight); Whole-soul turning toward being (provides the scope and goal of the
    required turning as movement of the whole soul from becoming to being)
Constructed meaning c06_1 (passage 7, inferred): Those fittest to govern must be compelled to leave contemplative blessedness and descend to share the prisoners' labours because neither the ignorant nor those who cling to endless contemplation will rule well on their own.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (establishes reluctance of those who attain
    vision to descend to human affairs); Islands of the Blest (characterizes endless study as
    fancy of dwelling apart in blessed isolation); Neither Extreme Can Rule (rules out both the
    ignorant and perpetual students as able ministers of State); Compelled Descent to Govern
    (imposes the founders' prescription to force the enlightened to descend and share labours
    and honours)
Constructed meaning c06_2 (passage 7, inferred): The same keen faculty that makes clever rogues acutely vicious when dragged downward by sensual weights would see truth just as keenly if freed early and turned upward.
    built from: Clever rogue's misdirected sight (shows keen sight persisting while forced into
    service of evil); Leaden Weights of Pleasure (identifies sensual pleasures as leaden weights
    dragging vision downward); Freed Faculty Sees Truth (asserts the same faculty would see
    truth as keenly if severed and turned upward in youth); Innate power of learning (grounds
    continuity in a capacity for learning already present in the soul)
Constructed meaning c07_1 (passage 8, inferred): The philosophers' very unwillingness to descend qualifies them as the most reluctant and therefore best rulers, so compelling their descent secures the best quietly governed state.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the initial unwillingness to descend
    and longing to dwell above); Reluctant Rulers Govern Best (provides the normative reversal
    that the most reluctant rulers make the best and most quietly governed state); Compelled
    Descent to Govern (provides the compelled descent that puts the unwilling into rule and
    harnesses their reluctance)
Constructed meaning c07_2 (passage 8, inferred): Compelling educated philosophers to descend and share rule is not unjust but owed service because they were bred as hive rulers for a legislation aimed at the happiness of the whole state rather than one class.
    built from: Happiness Of Whole State (provides the whole-state happiness aim that defines
    legislative purpose); Philosophers As Hive Rulers (provides the special debt that these
