Chained people who mistake shadows for truth are painfully dragged into sunlight toward the highest Good, then must learn to understand blindness and turn the soul so they will go back down to govern wisely.

1. Shadows and Painful Ascent  (passages 1-3)
    People chained since childhood mistake shadows for truth and suffer as they are dragged
    upward toward brighter realities culminating in the sun.
       -> Their dazzling climb sets up the shock of going back down to those who still trust
       shadows.
2. Blinded Return Reveals Good  (passages 4-4)
    When the freed prisoner returns, darkness blinds him, his old fellows mock and threaten him,
    and the story is explained as the soul's journey to the highest Good.
       -> This violent rejection demands a fairer way to judge confusion and excuse the
       returning philosopher.
3. Two Blindnesses and True Learning  (passages 5-6)
    Confusion can come from entering light or leaving it and should be diagnosed, while real
    education turns an inborn power toward reality rather than inserting knowledge.
       -> Seeing learning as turning the soul explains why the enlightened must be forced back
       down to help others.
4. Forced to Govern Justly  (passages 7-8)
    Those who would rather stay in blessed contemplation must be compelled to share rule because
    their reluctance makes them just and best for the whole community.
       -> Once compulsion is shown to be just, the question becomes how reluctant rulers can
       accept office willingly.
5. Accepting Rule Through Philosophy  (passages 9-9)
    Educated guardians take turns ruling as a hard duty, sustained by a better shared life of
    wisdom that despises ambition and keeps the city ordered.
